# CI/CD pipeline

`.gitlab-ci.yml` defines almost no jobs of its own. The lint, validate, test,
security and review jobs are `include`d from **{{ lib_project }}** at
`{{ lib_ref }}`, so a tooling change is made once in the library and picked up
here by moving that ref. Each template's inputs are documented in the library's
`docs/INCLUDE-CONTRACT.md`.

Only the **deploy** jobs are local: they are the part that touches this
cluster's hosts.

`include: project:` resolves on the GitLab instance the pipeline runs on, so
the library has to exist there as `{{ lib_project }}`. If this cluster does not
live on the instance that hosts the library, mirror or fork it and set
`lib_project` accordingly. `ansible/requirements.yml` and the Terraform module
sources are unaffected — they fetch over https from `{{ lib_url }}`.

## Stages

| Stage | Jobs | Source |
|---|---|---|
| `lint` | `yaml-lint`, `ansible-lint`, `shellcheck`, `python-lint`, `terraform-fmt`, `docs-link-check`, `flux-lint`, `version-check` (library); `version-coverage`, `repo-sync`, `deploy-coverage`, `netpol-parity`, `kustomization-coverage`, `cluster-literals`, `prometheus-config-lint`, `terraform-tflint`, `integration-matrix-coverage`, `flux-version-pin` (this repo) | mixed |
| `validate` | `terraform-validate` (library); `deploy-preflight`, `terraform-plan`, {% if vpn_tailscale %}`tailscale-drift-plan`, {% endif %}`authentik-drift-plan`{% if use_unifi %}, `unifi-drift-plan`{% endif %} (this repo) | mixed |
| `test` | `python-tests` (the cluster invariants in `tests/`, the suites beside the scripts in `scripts/`, the library-pin gate and the vendored-copy gate); `integration-tests` (the multi-role molecule stacks, `.gitlab/ci/integration-jobs.yml`) | mixed |
| `security` | `secret_detection` | library |
| `ai-review` | `pr-agent-review` | library |
| `gate` | `validation-gate` | this repo |
| `deploy` | `deploy-ansible-base`, `deploy-ansible-dns`, `deploy-ansible-storage`, `deploy-ansible-mail`, `deploy-ansible-k3s`, `deploy-terraform` | this repo |
| `verify` | `deploy-verify` (the postflight play, `when: always`), `cluster-verify` (the in-cluster half: server-side dry-run, forced Flux reconcile, Ready gates) | this repo |
| `maintenance` | `version-bump-bot` | library, scheduled |
{%- if enable_semantic_release %}
| `release` | `semantic-release` | library, last stage so every earlier job gates it |
{%- endif %}

Every job `.gitlab-ci.yml` defines locally is named in that table, and the
template's render gate fails when one is not — a new local job cannot ship
undocumented.

### Library inputs this repository overrides

Each include ships defaults; these are the ones this cluster changes and cannot
lose. A dropped override is silent — the job still runs, against different rules.

| Include | Override that is load-bearing |
|---|---|
| `yaml-lint` | `config: "-c lint/yamllint-relaxed.yml"`. The profile sits under `lint/` so ansible-lint cannot discover it as a repo-root `.yamllint` and replace its yaml rule. Without the input the job runs yamllint's shipped `-d relaxed`, which reports different findings than `task lint` does |
| `ansible-lint` | `image: python:3.13`, not the slim image: `ansible-galaxy` installs the collection from a `git+` source and needs `git` |
| `python-lint` | `targets: "scripts tests kubernetes"` — `kubernetes` is there for the Python a manifest delivers through a `configMapGenerator`. `ruff_version` is pinned as an input because include inputs resolve before job variables exist |
| `python-tests` | `setup_command` clones the library, which is what the vendored-copy gate compares against, and `changes:` names every path the suites read |
| `terraform` (`terraform-fmt`, `terraform-validate`) | `image` set to `$TF_JOB_IMAGE` — one exact Terraform patch for the lint jobs and the credentialed plan and apply jobs alike, so a plan cannot be refused by a different version |
| `flux-lint` | `substitute: true`, `flux_render_script` and `extra_validation`. These are what make the job mirror kustomize-controller instead of linting raw manifests |
| `version-check` | `setup_command` and `check_command` only; everything else is the library default |

Every one of them carries its reason in `.gitlab-ci.yml` beside the input. The
library's `docs/INCLUDE-CONTRACT.md` is the full list of what each include
accepts.

Nothing in `kubernetes/` is deployed by CI. Flux reconciles it from `main`;
`flux-lint` is the gate that keeps an unreconcilable commit off that branch.

### flux-lint

Runs in **substitute mode**: it mirrors what kustomize-controller does at
reconcile time. Both postBuild ConfigMaps —
`kubernetes/infrastructure/sources/versions-configmap.yaml` and
`kubernetes/infrastructure/sources/cluster-config.yaml` — are exported by
`scripts/flux-env.sh`, every `${placeholder}` in a built Kustomization must
resolve to one of their keys, and the substituted output is schema-validated
with kubeconform.

CR schemas come from the datreeio CRD catalog at one pinned revision.
`taskfiles/flux.yml` holds it as `CRD_CATALOG_REF`, matched to the library
`flux-lint` job's own default, so the pipeline and `task flux:lint` accept the
same schema set. It moves with `WEISSSRV_LIB_REF`: a bump that changes the job's
default needs the Taskfile var changed with it.

A placeholder that resolves to nothing is the failure this catches before
merge. In the cluster, strict substitution fails the Kustomization's reconcile
instead of applying a blank hostname.

`require_cluster_root` ships **false**, because
`kubernetes/clusters/{{ cluster_name }}/flux-system/` does not exist until
`task flux:bootstrap` writes it. Flip it to `true` afterwards: that is what
fails a cluster which has LOST its Flux content, rather than skipping the build.

Its `extra_validation` input then runs the cluster-invariant gates over that same
rendered corpus, each covering a class kubeconform structurally cannot see:

| Gate | Fails when |
|---|---|
| `check-hpa-vpa-invariant.py` | an HPA and a CPU-controlling VPA target one workload; a container declares a CPU limit; a container sets `requests.memory == limits.memory` under a memory-controlling VPA |
| `check-scrape-netpol.py` | a scraped namespace's NetworkPolicies do not admit Prometheus (the symptom is a bare `TargetDown`) |
| `check-default-deny-coverage.py` | a namespace owns a workload but carries no namespace-wide ingress default-deny (or one defeated by an allow-everything rule beside it) |
| `check-secretstore-scope.py` | a `ClusterSecretStore` has no `spec.conditions`, or a consumer namespace those conditions do not admit |
| `check-pvc-storageclass.py` | a claim omits `storageClassName`, which the DefaultStorageClass admission plugin rewrites at create time with no diff in git |
| `validate-helm-values.py` | a `.spec.values` block the kustomize corpus never renders fails `helm template` |

The last two arms run only with `--require-chart-native-vpas`, which
`scripts/flux-corpus-gates.sh` passes over the full corpus. A CPU limit buys
only CFS throttling, and a 1:1 memory ratio never gains headroom because the VPA
updater preserves it. Deliberate exceptions go in
`scripts/autoscaling-policy.yaml` under `cpu_limit_allowlist` and
`memory_ratio_allowlist`, each with a rationale.

`netpol-parity` has a `lint` job of its own because it reads the manifests on
disk rather than the corpus, so it also covers `kubernetes/clusters/*/flux-system/`,
and it compares every public-egress `except:` list against the canonical
reserved-CIDR sets. Deliberate exceptions live in `scripts/netpol-except.yaml`
with their reason.

That list is not written twice. `extra_validation` calls
`scripts/flux-corpus-gates.sh`, and so does `task flux:lint`, so the two cannot
drift: add a gate to that script and both sides pick it up.
`task lint:netpol-parity` covers the disk-reading one, and `task lint` runs
both.

`scripts/flux-render.sh` is the library's copy, vendored unmodified (the
vendored-copy gate below checks it byte-for-byte). The multi-ConfigMap handling
lives in the local wrapper `scripts/flux-env.sh`, which is what both CI and
`task flux:lint` invoke.

