#### What `test_cluster_invariants.py` asserts

- No duplicate address or vmid in the inventory, and no address outside
  `cluster_lan_cidr`.
- Every `${placeholder}` in `kubernetes/` is a key one of the two ConfigMaps
  defines, and no manifest carries a site literal instead.
- Every playbook is either wired to a deploy job or acknowledged in
  `scripts/deploy-coverage.conf`.

Each suite's `changes:` rule is derived from what it reads: `test_lib_pins.py`
from `ansible/requirements.yml`, `Taskfile.yml` and `terraform/*/*.tf`;
`test_cluster_invariants.py` from `hosts.yml`, `.copier-answers.yml`,
`ansible/playbooks/**` and `scripts/deploy-coverage.conf`. Widen the rule in the
same commit that widens what a suite reads.

The vendored-copy gate reads [`scripts/vendored-manifest.yml`](../scripts/vendored-manifest.yml)
— **this repository's** list of what it copies from `{{ lib_project }}` — and
runs the library's own `check-vendored-copies.py` engine over it. It covers the
`scripts/` copies plus the lint profiles and dotfiles no listing of that
directory reaches, each declared a **fork** in that manifest with a reason and
the library blob it was last reconciled against. Read the `forked:` list in the
manifest rather than a copy of it here.

That is why this job clones the library: `setup_command` fetches it at
`variables.WEISSSRV_LIB_REF` into `$WEISSSRV_LIB_PATH` with the job token, so a
private library works as long as it lists this project on its CI/CD job-token
allowlist. The gate **never skips** — an unreachable checkout fails the job,
because a copy nobody compares is exactly the drift it exists to catch.

Locally the same suite runs as `task lint:invariants` (part of `task lint`)
against the `.weisssrv-lib/` checkout `task lib:sync` creates. Refreshing a
copy is `task lib:sync`, `cp`, review the diff — never an edit in place, which
the next refresh silently reverts.

### integration-tests

The multi-role molecule stacks under `ansible/integration-tests/` run as one
`parallel:matrix` job defined in `.gitlab/ci/integration-jobs.yml`. That job has
its own file so its `changes:` rule can name a path only it lives in: naming
`.gitlab-ci.yml` instead would fire the whole privileged fan-out on every
CI-only edit.

`scripts/check-integration-matrix-coverage.py` is the gate that keeps the two in
step. A scenario directory with no matrix entry never runs and reads as green,
because nothing executed it. It runs as `integration-matrix-coverage` in `lint`
and as `task lint:integration-matrix` locally.

#### A scenario container dies at prepare: "Too many open files"

The container is created, then systemd PID 1 inside it exits with `Failed to
create control group inotify object: Too many open files`.
`fs.inotify.max_user_instances` is per-UID and host-global, so the kernel default
of 128 is exhausted on a container-dense node and the next systemd-in-Docker
container cannot start. It is intermittent, hits at any concurrency, and clusters
on one node at a time.

The `k3s` role pins `fs.inotify.max_user_instances = 8192` and
`fs.inotify.max_user_watches = 1048576` on every node through
`/etc/sysctl.d/90-k3s-inotify.conf` (the `k3s_inotify_*` role defaults). Read the
live value on the node the runner landed on:

```bash
ssh <k3s-node> cat /proc/sys/fs/inotify/max_user_instances
```

128 means the drop-in is missing or was never applied; `task k3s:deploy`
re-asserts it.

### docs-link-check

`scripts/check-doc-links.py` resolves every relative `.md` link in the docs, so
a renamed or deleted page fails here rather than in a reader's browser. It runs
as `docs-link-check` in `lint` and as `task lint:doc-links` locally.

A second arm that also resolves the repository paths the docs cite in backticks
arrives with the next library release.

### version-coverage

`check-versions.py --check-coverage` compares `scripts/version-registry.py`
against the pins in `group_vars/all.yml`. A pin the registry does not list is
never checked and never mentioned, so a pin with no upstream to check belongs in
the registry's `untracked_allowlist` **with a rationale** — never in neither
place. The job needs no credentials and no network, which is why it sits in
`lint` rather than waiting for the weekly bot.

### repo-sync

Three files in this repository are generated, and this job regenerates each into
a temporary directory and diffs it against what is committed: `scripts/hosts.env`
from the inventory, the cluster-versions ConfigMap from `group_vars/all.yml`, and
`kubernetes/infrastructure/observability/loki/host-log-staleness.yaml` from the
`alloy_host` hosts. Nothing else compares a generated file against its source, so
without this job a stale copy ships. `task lint:repo-sync` is the same three
checks locally.

Two arms are red on a fresh cluster by design: `scripts/hosts.env` ships as a
placeholder and host-log-staleness carries the cluster-wide alert only. Bring-up
step 3 clears both, and the job names each arm separately, so a genuinely stale
file is still readable in the output.

### flux-version-pin

Flux is pinned in three places: `FLUX_VERSION` in the `cluster-verify` job (the
CLI it downloads, with its sha256), `flux_version` in the cluster-versions
ConfigMap, and the `# Flux Version:` header of
`kubernetes/clusters/{{ cluster_name }}/flux-system/gotk-components.yaml`, which
`flux bootstrap` wrote. `scripts/check-flux-version-pin.py` compares them, and
also checks that the manifest still lists the four controllers.

A CLI newer than the committed manifest is the failure this catches: it installs
a control plane nobody reviewed. Bumping Flux is therefore five edits in one
change — the job's version and sha256, `group_vars/all.yml`, `task
flux:sync-versions`, `flux install --export` with the matching CLI, and
confirming the feature-gate names patched onto the controllers in
`kubernetes/clusters/{{ cluster_name }}/flux-system/kustomization.yaml` are still
recognised by the new controller — an unknown gate makes kustomize-controller
exit at startup.

Before `task flux:bootstrap` there is no `gotk-components.yaml`, and the gate
checks the two files that do exist. `task lint:flux-version-pin` is the same
check locally.

### deploy-preflight

`scripts/deploy-preflight.py` walks every job extending `.deploy-base` or
`.maintenance-base`, checks that each `ansible-playbook` call names a playbook
that exists, and runs `--list-tasks` for each `--tags` selection to prove it
reaches at least one task. A tag that matches nothing makes a deploy job a green
no-op, which nothing else in the pipeline can see, because the play still exits
0. It also fails when it cannot parse a job's invocation, so a rewritten job
cannot quietly drop out of coverage. `task lint:deploy-preflight` runs the same
script.

### Drift plans

`terraform-plan` and the `*-drift-plan` jobs are read-only: they report a
difference between this repository and the live provider and never apply one.
They run `terraform plan -detailed-exitcode`, so **exit 2 is advisory drift**
(`allow_failure` admits it) and **exit 1 is a broken plan**, which turns the job
red. The drift plans read live credentials, so they run on `main` and on a
schedule, never on a merge request.{% if use_unifi %} `unifi-drift-plan` also
needs a LAN-reachable runner: the console is not published, so a runner
elsewhere fails at the first data read.{% endif %}

### The verify stage

`deploy-verify` and `cluster-verify` are their own stage after `deploy`, not
deploy-stage jobs with `needs:` on the deploys. A job that declares any
`needs:` ignores stage order, and a need that was created and then failed
blocks it — `optional: true` only tolerates a need that was never created. So
both jobs carry no `needs:` at all and run `when: always`, which is what makes
verification run after a failed deploy rather than being skipped with it.

`when: always` is pipeline-wide, though. On a `main` pipeline that fails at lint
while matching a deploy job's `changes:`, `validation-gate` blocks the deploys
and `deploy-verify` still runs, verifying a deployment that was never
attempted. It is read-only, so nothing breaks, but it is a second failure
surface on an already-red pipeline.

## Secret detection

`secret_detection` scans the pipeline's **commit range only**:
`SECRET_DETECTION_HISTORIC_SCAN` stays false. A full-history pass is that
variable set to `true` on a pipeline schedule. The one-time local audit is:

```bash
gitleaks detect --source . --config .gitleaks.toml --log-opts='--all'
```

A false positive gets a content-shape or rule-scoped allowlist entry in
`.gitleaks.toml`, which `.gitlab/secret-detection-ruleset.toml` passes through.
Never use a path exclusion, and never override
`SECRET_DETECTION_EXCLUDED_PATHS` inline in `.gitlab-ci.yml`: that disables the
gate for a whole tree with no record of why.

## Prerequisites

### Runner

Every job is tagged `{{ runner_tag }}`. `flux-lint` (substitute mode), every job
in the `deploy` stage and both `verify`-stage jobs, `deploy-verify` and
`cluster-verify`, need a **root-capable** runner: they `apt-get install` and read
secrets with the 1Password CLI. If you split runners, keep those on the
privileged one; the rest are non-root safe.

`secret_detection` pins `KUBERNETES_NODE_SELECTOR_CPU` to
`{{ ci_cpu_selector }}` — gitleaks
needs POPCNT/SSE4.2 and crashes on older CPUs. The value must satisfy the
runner's `node_selector_overwrite_allowed` regex, or the job fails at pod
creation; a runner that sets no regex ignores it.

### CI/CD variables

**Masked and Protected are different controls.** Masked only hides a value in
job logs. **Protected** is what stops a feature branch from reading it at all:
without it, anyone who can open a merge request can print the whole-vault token
by adding a line to `.gitlab-ci.yml` in that MR. Set Protected on every
credential that could move state, and protect `main`
([SETUP.md](https://github.com/ericsweiss/weisssrv-cluster-template/blob/main/docs/SETUP.md))
so the setting means something.

| Variable | Used by | Protected | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN` | every deploy job | **Yes** | Masked, and the rule gate for the whole deploy stage — see below |
| `VERSION_BUMP_BOT_TOKEN` | `version-bump-bot` | **Yes** | Masked PAT, `api` + `write_repository` — a job token can neither push nor open an MR |
| `OPENAI__KEY` | `pr-agent-review` | No | Masked. The reviewer runs ON merge requests, so a protected variable would disable it. Scope the key to a spend limit instead. |
| `GITLAB__PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` | `pr-agent-review` | No | Masked, `api` scope — the account the review comments post as. Same reason as above; give it the least privilege that can comment. |
| `GH_API_TOKEN` | `version-bump-bot` | **Yes** | Optional, masked, read-only with no scopes — raises the GitHub API rate limit the version checker runs into (`GITHUB_TOKEN` is accepted as a fallback alias) |

`version-bump-bot` runs only from a schedule or a web run on `main`, so its PAT
must be Protected as well as Masked.

Every deploy rule is gated on `$OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN`, so a fork with no
token gets a green pipeline that creates no deploy jobs rather than a broken
one. Protecting it also removes `terraform-plan` from merge requests — the
trade-off is under "Deploy jobs".

### {{ onepassword_vault }} vault items

The five Ansible deploy jobs are abbreviated below by their suffix — `base`,
`dns`, `storage`, `mail`, `k3s`.

The deploy jobs read these at job time with `op`:

| Item / field | Used by |
|---|---|
| `SSH Key` → `private key`, `public key` | every Ansible deploy — `.deploy-base` reads the private key Ansible connects with, each job passes the public one to the user role |
| `Loki Push Auth` → `username`, `password` | every Ansible deploy — `.deploy-base` exports it so a log-shipping role never renders empty credentials |
| `Email Config` → `root_alias` | `base`, `dns`, `mail`, `k3s` |
| `SMTP Relay Auth` → `username`, `password` | `base`, `dns`, `mail`, `k3s` (senders authenticating TO the relay) |
| `SMTP Smarthost` → `username`, `password` | `base`, `mail` (the relay authenticating to its upstream) |
| `AdGuard Home` → `password` | `base`, `dns` |
| `Cert Distribution Key` → `private key`, `public key` | `base`, `dns` |
| `Samba NAS User` → `password` | `base`, `storage` |
{% if vpn_tailscale %}| `Tailscale Auth Key` → `credential` | `base` |
{% endif %}| `K3s Cluster Token` / `K3s Agent Token` → `credential` | `k3s` |
| `K3s Kubeconfig` → `kubeconfig` | `cluster-verify` — base64, read by the library's kubectl-setup fragment |
| `Cloudflare Terraform Token` → `credential`, `username` | `terraform-plan`, `deploy-terraform` |
{% if vpn_tailscale %}| `Tailscale OAuth` → `client id`, `credential` | `tailscale-drift-plan` |
{% endif %}| `Authentik Terraform Token` → `credential` | `authentik-drift-plan` |
| `<App> SSO` → the OIDC client secret | `authentik-drift-plan`, one per application |
{% if use_unifi %}| `UniFi Controller` → `url`, `api-key` | `unifi-drift-plan` |
| `WiFi IoT` / `WiFi Guest` → `password` | `unifi-drift-plan`, one per WLAN |
{% endif %}
`deploy-ansible-base` runs `site.yml`, the whole host stack, so it reads the
union of what the group jobs do.

Each deploy job's `op://` variables mirror the `env:` of the matching Taskfile
task, and that correspondence is load-bearing: `op run` resolves **every**
`op://` reference in the environment, so a reference to an item the vault does
not hold fails the job even when the playbook never reads it. Declare what the
playbook needs and nothing more.

Everything else a playbook needs is an `op://` reference in
`ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/all.yml`, resolved by `op run --`.

## Deploys

Deploy jobs run on `main` only, and only when their own paths changed, so a
merge deploys exactly what it touched. `deploy-ansible-base` runs `site.yml` on
a cluster-wide inventory or playbook change; `deploy-ansible-dns`,
`-storage` and `-mail` are the fast path for a change scoped to one group's
`group_vars` or playbook. All four run automatically. `deploy-ansible-k3s` and
`deploy-terraform` are **manual** — both can disrupt the control plane or public
DNS, so an operator triggers them after reading the diff.

They are `interruptible: false` (a new pipeline must never cancel a running
deploy) and retry twice on `runner_system_failure` only — an evicted runner pod
is environmental and the playbooks are idempotent.

### Cancellation policy

The `default:` block sets `interruptible: true`, and `workflow.auto_cancel` sets
`on_new_commit: interruptible` — so a new push to a merge request cancels the
previous pipeline's jobs instead of letting them pile up on the runner. The
`main` rule overrides that to `none`: a merged pipeline always runs to
completion. Without the override, a second merge cancels the first's still-
running jobs, which skips `validation-gate` and leaves the first merge's deploys
unmet and never run — with its `changes:` not re-covered by the second pipeline.
Rapid merges instead queue at the deploy jobs' `resource_group`s.

`.deploy-base`, `validation-gate`, `terraform-plan` and `deploy-terraform`
override `interruptible` back to `false`, so none of them is ever cancellable on
either ref.

A **cancelled** main pipeline is the one case the verify stage does not cover.
Cancelling cancels pending jobs rather than running them, so `deploy-verify`
never executes and a partly-applied deploy stage goes unverified. Recovery is to
re-run the pipeline, or `task infra:deploy` — not to assume it converged.

The deploy jobs' `resource_group`s serialize them, but a group's `process_mode`
is an API-only setting that is not in this repository and comes back at its
default of `unordered` after a project restore. Unordered lets queued deploys
run out of submission order, which is exactly what the queueing is there to
prevent. Every group must be `oldest_first`; check them all after any restore,
because a partial checklist leaves the rest unordered:

```bash
glab api projects/<path>/resource_groups
glab api -X PUT projects/<path>/resource_groups/<name> -f process_mode=oldest_first
```

### The validation gate

Every deploy job carries `needs: [validation-gate]`, non-optionally. That single
job in the `gate` stage lists every lint/validate/test/security job as an
**optional** need, plus `secret_detection`, which has no path filter and so
always exists on a `main` pipeline.

The indirection is not decoration. A job that declares `needs:` is no longer
ordered by stage, and GitLab **drops** an `optional: true` entry whose job the
rules excluded — so a deploy job whose only needs were optional lint jobs ends
up with an empty needs set and starts immediately, before those jobs run and
regardless of whether they later fail. Pointing every deploy at one job that
always exists is what keeps the needs set non-empty and the stage order real.

`validation-gate` must therefore be created for every pipeline that creates a
deploy job: its rules are the deploy rules minus the `$OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN`
clause (it needs no credentials). A deploy job needing a gate that was not
created fails pipeline **creation**, so keep the two in step when you change
either set of rules.

The drift plans and `pr-agent-review` are deliberately not listed: they allow
failure, and an allow-failure job counts as successful for a `needs:`, so
listing them would gate nothing.

`deploy-terraform` applies the plan `terraform-plan` saved earlier in the same
pipeline, not a fresh one, so what ships is what the merge request's Terraform
widget showed. `terraform-plan` is the only job that reads the secret store on a
merge-request ref; marking `OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN` protected removes the plan
from merge requests, and a pipeline with no token produces no plan at all —
that is a skip, not a clean plan.

### Drift plans

The {% if vpn_tailscale %}tailnet ACL and the {% endif %}SSO objects are applied by
hand (`task terraform:*-apply` refuses `-auto-approve`), so nothing would
otherwise notice an admin-console edit. The drift jobs re-plan read-only on a
schedule and post-merge, and exit 2 on a non-empty diff. Each scopes that with
`allow_failure: exit_codes: [2]`, so drift warns while a broken detector — a
plan error, expired credentials, an unreachable endpoint, a state lock — exits 1
and fails red. They carry no merge-request rule: that would materialise vault
secrets into a job running an unmerged branch's own code.

A drift exit 2 leaves the pipeline **passed with warnings**, and GitLab sends no
mail for a warned pipeline. Nothing tells you about drift unless you watch the
scheduled pipeline yourself in **Settings → CI/CD → Schedules** (§ Version
bumps) or wire a pipeline-event webhook.

The branch name is written **literally** as `main`: GitLab does not expand
variables inside a quoted `rules:if`, so `$CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH` would be compared
as text and match nothing. If your default branch is not `main`, change these
rules and pass the library templates' `default_branch` input to match —
otherwise the deploy jobs, and the library's own default-branch-only jobs,
silently stop running.

### Adding one

The six jobs above are the shape every deploy job follows. A playbook of your
own gets one more; `<name>` below is yours to fill in, and must not collide with
a job the pipeline already defines:

```yaml
deploy-ansible-<name>:
  stage: deploy
  extends: .deploy-base
  resource_group: deploy-ansible-<name>       # serialize runs of THIS job
  needs:
    - job: validation-gate                    # NOT optional — see above
    - job: deploy-ansible-base                # ordering only, so: optional
      optional: true
  variables:                                  # only what THIS playbook needs
    SSH_PUBLIC_KEY: "op://{{ onepassword_vault }}/SSH Key/public key"
  script:
    - cd ansible
    - $SECRET_RUNNER ansible-playbook -i inventories/prod playbooks/<name>.yml
  environment:
    name: production/ansible-<name>
  rules:
    - if: '$CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE == "schedule"'
      when: never
    - if: '$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == "main" && $OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN'
      changes:
        - "ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/<name>.yml"
        - "ansible/playbooks/<name>.yml"
```

`$SECRET_RUNNER` is what resolves the `op://` references, so the playbook is
always invoked through it, never bare.

`.deploy-base` comes from the library (`/ci/deploy/deploy-base.yml`, included
with this cluster's runner tag and vault name). It supplies the 1Password CLI,
`git` (ansible-galaxy installs the collection from a `git+` source), the pinned
Ansible, a secure `ansible.cfg` copy, the SSH key, an `ssh-keyscan` of every
inventory host (from `scripts/hosts.env`), the galaxy install of
`weisssrv.infra`, and the `LOKI_PUSH_*` references every log-shipping role
needs. A job only declares what its OWN playbook adds.

The `changes:` list is the contract: a role that a playbook runs but whose path
is not listed here goes green on every change while never actually deploying.
When a playbook gains a role, extend the list in the same commit.

{% if git_backend == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}## Runner reaper partial sweep

`GitlabRunnerReaperPartialSweep` fires when the reaper CronJob logged a
`BUDGET STOP`: it exited cleanly without reaping every runner namespace, so
orphan CI job pods and their registry-credential Secrets keep accumulating
against the namespace quota.

First move: read the CronJob logs for the namespaces the sweep skipped.

```bash
kubectl -n gitlab-runner-reaper logs -l job-name --tail=200 | grep -E 'BUDGET STOP|swept'
```

Compare those namespaces' pod counts against the quota, then either raise
`BUDGET_SECONDS` so a sweep finishes, or shorten `MAX_AGE_MINUTES` so each sweep
has less to delete. Both live in
`kubernetes/apps/gitlab-runner-reaper/cronjob.yaml`. If the counts are flat and
the sweep still runs out of budget, the apiserver is the bottleneck, not the
reaper.

{% endif %}## Version bumps

`version-check` is the read-only half. It reports which pins have an update
available; it opens nothing. It writes `version-report.json` as a job artifact.
Nothing posts the report to the merge request. A wrapper that does is a local
extension and needs a Reporter+api CI token; it is not part of the include.
`version-check` itself needs no token and no schedule: it runs on every merge
request and every push whose paths match its `changes:` filter (three paths
here, so most merge requests do not run it), as a manual button on web
pipelines, and on any scheduled pipeline. It is soft-fail, so "updates
available" never reds a pipeline.

`version-bump-bot` runs **only from a pipeline schedule on `main`** (or a
manual web run on it). It is the non-interactive twin of `task maintenance:update-all-versions`:
it clones the pinned library, runs its `check-versions.py --update-all` against
`scripts/version-registry.py`, regenerates the versions ConfigMap, and keeps
exactly one MR on `bot/version-bumps` in sync — new bumps force-push and create
or refresh the MR, unchanged content does nothing, and no bumps closes an open
MR. It never merges.

The bot's command list and the Taskfile's must stay in step; CI has no go-task,
which is why the commands are spelled out in `.gitlab-ci.yml` rather than
invoking the task. A pin the registry does not list is never checked — a fresh
cluster's registry is empty, so the bot correctly does nothing until you add
entries.

Two pipeline schedules, both targeting `main`, both created in **Settings →
CI/CD → Schedules**. A `SCHEDULE_TYPE` variable selects which opt-in job runs.
Pick the cron and the timezone to suit your own maintenance window; nothing in
the repository asserts them:

| Cadence | `SCHEDULE_TYPE` | What it adds |
|---|---|---|
| Weekly | `version-bump` | `version-bump-bot` |
| Nightly | `full-test` | `integration-tests`, unfiltered, as an external-dependency canary for upstream image and package rot |

The advisory drift-plan jobs run on any schedule and need no variable. Every
other job carries a `schedule → never` rule.

The report the checker writes (`version-report.json`) stays untracked and is
embedded in the MR description rather than committed.

## Bumping the library

Change every `ref:` in `.gitlab-ci.yml`, the `version:` in
`ansible/requirements.yml`, the `?ref=` on each Terraform module source and
`LIB_REF` in `Taskfile.yml` to the new tag, in one MR, and record it in
`.copier-answers.yml` by re-running `copier update`. All four are gated:
`scripts/check-lib-pins.py` (and `--fix`) covers the `include:` entries, and
`tests/test_lib_pins.py` covers the other three — a stale `version:` in
`requirements.yml` is the one that keeps the pipeline green while every deploy
job installs the OLD role code. There is no dependency bot for it: a library
bump can change job behaviour and role interfaces, so it is deliberate. `docs/RUNBOOKS.md` § The platform library is the full procedure,
including the re-initialisation each Terraform module needs afterwards.

The pin in git is the record of what was validated. A pipeline proves this
cluster state against the tag in `variables.WEISSSRV_LIB_REF` at that commit,
and the gates hold `ansible/requirements.yml`, each Terraform `?ref=` and the
Taskfile's `LIB_REF` equal to it. After a rollback,
`git show <sha>:.gitlab-ci.yml` names the library release that state was tested
against, so no separate table is kept.

## Backend seams

The pipeline assumes two of the choices made when this repository was
generated:

- **`secrets_backend: {{ secrets_backend }}`** — `.deploy-base` installs the
  1Password CLI and reads the SSH key with `op read`, playbooks run under
  `$SECRET_RUNNER`, and `$OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN` gates every deploy rule.
  Nothing in `.gitlab-ci.yml` spells a backend-native reference inline: the
  `secret_ref` / `secret_read` Jinja macros at the top of the file write them,
  and `SECRET_RUNNER` is the command that resolves them. Another backend needs a
  branch in each of those, the library deploy fragment replaced with one that
  fetches its credentials, a way to resolve the references in the inventory, and
  a variable to gate the deploy rules on.
- **`git_backend: {{ git_backend }}`** — the file is GitLab CI. A GitHub
  Actions equivalent would need workflows reproducing these jobs; the library
  ships GitLab CI templates only, so the shared half would have to be rebuilt
  as composite actions. Everything under `.gitlab/` is GitLab-only in the same
  way and carries no backend arm: the child-pipeline include, the
  secret-detection ruleset and the issue and merge-request skeletons render for
  every answer, because `gitlab_selfhosted` is the only implemented forge.
